  47: ## FEA so far
  48: 
  49: The frame model is one merged body with 20 A- 20 A- 2 mm tube for each 2020, so joints are perfectly bonded. Real corner brackets and T-nuts are softer and can slip. Treat frame deflections as lower bounds, and use percentiles rather than the peaks, which sit at sharp corners. Frame material is 6063-T5, yield 145 MPa, assumed.
  50: 
  51: | Case | Load | Applied at |
  52: | --- | --- | --- |
  53: | Peak | Each arm 60 NA�m pitch plus 330 N down (279 N from the torque, 51 N from 2 A- gravity) | Arm joint line, X A�208.49. Base fixed |
  54: | Tip-over, side | 5.3 kN lower body, 1.2 kN far arm | Near box side fixed |
  55: | Tip-over, forward | 5.3 kN lower body | Both box sides fixed |
  56: 
  57: **Tip-over basis:** ground-level fall from standing, 40 kg (assumed), about 0.75 m centre-of-mass drop, 294 J. About 60% arrives at first contact, stopped over 40 mm of shell and link crush, with a peak of 1.5 A- average. That gives about 6.6 kN, or 17 g. It scales inversely with crush distance, so 80 mm halves it.
  58: 
  59: ### Frame
  60: 
  61: | Case | Gusset | Displacement, mm | Peak, MPa | 99th percentile, MPa |
  62: | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |
  63: | Peak | 3 mm aluminium | 0.08 | 22.7 | 7 |
  64: | Peak | None | 0.08 | 25.6 | 10 |
  65: | Tip-over, side | 3 mm aluminium | 1.8 | 297 | 100 |
  66: | Tip-over, side | None | 5.3 | 434 | 196 |
  67: | Tip-over, forward | 3 mm aluminium | 1.8 | 277 | 117 |
  68: | Tip-over, forward | None | 3.1 | 429 | 168 |
  69: 
  70: Normal use does not need the gusset. The tip-over does: without it, the 99th percentile is over yield.
  71: 
  72: ### Plate alone
  73: 
  74: One plate carrying the whole share, with no help from the shared rail. In-plane stress scales with 1/thickness, so the aluminium rows are scaled from the 10 mm run, not solved. Printed flat, layers parallel to the plate.
  75: 
  76: | Plate | Case | Peak, MPa | Safety factor | Layer safety factor | Displacement, mm |
  77: | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |
  78: | 10 mm PETG | Peak | 17.0 | 2.6 | 3.2 | 0.55 |
  79: | 10 mm PETG | Tip-over | 36.9 | 1.2 | 1.5 | 0.43 |
  80: | 3 mm 6061-T6 | Tip-over, scaled | 123 | 2.2 | | 0.04 |
  81: | 4 mm 6061-T6 | Tip-over, scaled | 92 | 3.0 | | 0.03 |
  82: 
  83: Bolts in the tip-over: 4.3 kN per plate over 5 bolts is about 860 N each. Bearing is 17 MPa on 10 mm PETG and 57 MPa on 3 mm aluminium. Friction on an M5 T-nut is only about 1 kN, assumed, and clamping through PETG relaxes, so printed plates are likely to slip onto the bolts.
  84: 
  85: **Printed plates survive but do not protect the frame.** 10 mm PETG has about 9% of the in-plane stiffness of 3 mm aluminium, so the frame still takes nearly the no-gusset load. Estimated side displacement is about 5 mm against 1.8 mm. **Recommendation: 3 mm 6061-T6 in the same outline**, with a larger radius at the X 113, Y 335 corner, where the 297 MPa peak sits.
  86: 
