 138: ## Tip-over against crush distance
 139: 
 140: The shell only has 8 to 17 mm to the frame at the back and sides, so 40 mm of crush was optimistic. Linear scaling from the 3 mm aluminium runs. Limit used: 99th percentile at 145 MPa yield.
 141: 
 142: Back case: frame held on the rear rails of the upper box, 5.3 kN of lower body, 3.2 mm, 99th percentile 146 MPa. See `STEPPED_FALL_BACK_3MM.png`. Holding one face is harsher than a real landing on the whole shell.
 143: 
 144: | Stop | Peak factor | Load | Side, 99th pct | Back, 99th pct | Holds? |
 145: | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |
 146: | 10 mm shell gap | 1.5 | 26 kN | 400 MPa | 584 MPa | No |
 147: | 10 mm gap + 10 mm TPU | 1.2 | 10.6 kN | 160 MPa | 234 MPa | No, local yield |
 148: | 20 mm of rigid crush | 1.5 | 13 kN | 200 MPa | 292 MPa | No |
 149: | 30 mm | 1.5 | 8.8 kN | 133 MPa | 194 MPa | Side only |
 150: | 40 mm | 1.5 | 6.6 kN | 100 MPa | 146 MPa | Side yes, back at the limit |
 151: 
 152: The frame needs about 30 mm of stop at the side and 40 mm at the back. A side fall lands on the arm first, which stands more than 100 mm proud of the frame, so 30 mm is reachable there if the printed arm parts crush. The back has nothing proud of it, so it needs a pad. Bolted joints will slip or tear before these stresses matter, so treat 2020 and its T-nuts as parts to inspect after any fall.
